<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basses-Pyrénées</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Pyrénées-Atlantiques</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basses-Pyrénées was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> created in 1790 from Béarn, Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labourd">Labourd</a> and Soule, with Pau as its capital. It covered the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and the French Basque Country on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In 1969 it was renamed Pyrénées-Atlantiques.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees.jpg" alt="Basses-Pyrénées" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tangopaso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-P%C3%A9e-sur-Nivelle_(lac),_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
